Remark `rem:mechanism` claims that '$\cd_{\VC[C]}N$ grows at least like $\cd(W_C)$' and that the pushout 'yields a Bredon Mayer–Vietoris sequence relating $\cd_{\VC}\Gamma$ to the strata $\cd_{\VC[C]}N_\Gamma[C]$ … the upshot is that $\cd_{\VC}\Gamma$ is at least of the order of $\sup_{[C]}\cd(W_C)$.' These statements are asserted with no proof, and the remark itself concedes it is purely motivational.

If one wishes to make part (2) self-contained (closing the central gap), this is exactly the chain that must be made rigorous:
(a) the Lück–Weiermann $\Gamma$-pushout (Theorem `thm:LW`) induces a Bredon-cohomology Mayer–Vietoris long exact sequence;
(b) for each commensurability class $[C]$, a lower bound $\cd_{\VC[C]}N_\Gamma[C] \ge \cd(W_C)$ via the quotient $N_\Gamma[C]\to W_C=N_\Gamma[C]/C$ collapsing the single commensurability stratum;
(c) survival of these unbounded-dimension classes in the Mayer–Vietoris sequence (the $E\Gamma$ term and the gluing terms have bounded dimension), forcing $\cd_{\VC}\Gamma=\infty$ whenever $\sup_{[C]}\cd(W_C)=\infty$.

None of (a)–(c) is established. This is the quantitative skeleton underlying the black-box theorem.
